import re

p6_prose = """<p>Long-Term Potentiation (LTP)—a persistent, activity-dependent strengthening of chemical synapses—represents the primary cellular and molecular model of learning and declarative memory formation. Classically characterized at the Schaffer collateral–CA1 pyramidal cell synapse in the hippocampus, LTP fulfills Donald Hebb's postulate that coordinated, coincident firing between presynaptic and postsynaptic neurons selectively consolidates synaptic efficacy ("cells that fire together wire together").</p>
<p>The induction of classic LTP relies on postsynaptic ionotropic glutamate receptors: $\\alpha$-amino-3-hydroxy-5-methyl-4-isoxazolepropionic acid (AMPA) receptors and $N$-methyl-$D$-aspartate (NMDA) receptors. Under resting membrane potentials (-70 mV), AMPA receptors mediate basal fast excitatory postsynaptic potentials (EPSPs) via $\\text{Na}^+$ influx, whereas NMDA receptor channel pores are electrostatically plugged by extracellular magnesium ($\\text{Mg}^{2+}$) ions. When high-frequency stimulation (HFS; tetanus) produces sustained, repetitive AMPA activation, the resulting robust postsynaptic depolarization expels the $\\text{Mg}^{2+}$ plug. This allows the NMDA receptor to act as a molecular coincidence detector: opening only when presynaptic glutamate release coincides with postsynaptic depolarization, permitting a massive influx of calcium ($\\text{Ca}^{2+}$) into dendritic spines.</p>
<p>Intracellular $\\text{Ca}^{2+}$ binds calmodulin, activating $\\text{Ca}^{2+}$/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase II (CaMKII). During Early-LTP (E-LTP; lasting 1–3 hours), CaMKII phosphorylates existing AMPA receptors (enhancing single-channel conductance) and drives insertion of reserve AMPA receptors into the postsynaptic density (PSD). In contrast, Late-LTP (L-LTP; persisting for many hours or days) requires activation of adenylyl cyclase, protein kinase A (PKA), and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK), which phosphorylate the transcription factor CREB to initiate de novo gene expression and local dendritic protein synthesis.</p>
<p>To characterize the molecular mechanisms governing E-LTP and L-LTP, electrophysiologists recorded field excitatory postsynaptic potential (fEPSP) slopes in acute rodent hippocampal CA1 slices following high-frequency tetanus (100 Hz, 1 s) across five pharmacological conditions (Table 1).</p>"""

p_texts = re.findall(r"<p>(.*?)</p>", p6_prose, re.DOTALL)
prose = re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", " ".join(p_texts))
words = prose.split()
print("Word count:", len(words))
